{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: I have the shape, and it is smaller than either option you offered. Recording it before I build, because it changes what counts as a valid transfer.\n\nTWO FACTS I CHECKED FIRST. internal/custody has no production callers -- only its own tests and spike/cmd/gitseq-report name it -- so this is the example application and the blast radius of a contract change is the example itself. And the package doc comment at the top of Fold already says \"logs establish identity, ordering and causal references; this projection decides whether those facts transfer custody\". The contract already claims ordering comes from the logs. The code does not honour it; it uses the caller's slice index as a silent stand-in. So this is not a contract change, it is the code catching up with the sentence above it.\n\nWHY POSITION ALONE DOES NOT WORK, which I worked out before reaching for it. Give Record a per-log position and you can order two settlements from the same log, so A to D is provably after A to B. But B to C lives in B's log and there is no position comparable across logs. Case one then still ends in an incomparable pair, and I would be back to disputing an honest chain, having added a field for nothing.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY ORDERS CUSTODY IS CUSTODY. B can only offer B to C because B holds the asset, and B holds it because settle A to B said so. If a transfer is required to cite the settlement that gave its offeror custody, the ordering stops needing to be inferred: the chain is written down. Custody becomes a linked list in the log rather than something a fold reconstructs from slice order. The initial owner's first transfer cites nothing, and there can be exactly one of those.\n\nWHAT THAT GIVES, checked against your three cases rather than asserted. Case one: A to B cites the genesis position, B to C cites settle A to B, and A to D cites either nothing or the same genesis A to B already spent, so it is ineffective for want of a live custody basis. Resolves at C, and A to D is refused on its own evidence rather than by a look-ahead. Case two, the reacquisition: B to A cites settle A to B, A to C cites settle B to A, unbroken chain, resolves at C. Case three, permutation: the answer is carried by citations, not by slice index, so reversing the input cannot change it. Competition becomes exactly what it should be -- two settlements citing the SAME custody basis, which is a real double-spend and is genuinely disputed -- and the quadratic look-ahead disappears entirely rather than being optimised, which is your simplification point resolved rather than deferred.\n\nIT FAILS CLOSED. No citation, no authority. A saga that does not name where its offeror's custody came from is ineffective, not merely unordered.\n\nI am building this under promise 5db5bfba. It is a bigger change than exclude-non-owner-sagas: Body or the citation rule gains a custody basis, the pre-existing disputed test's fixture has to grow citations to keep meaning what it means, and I will say in the artifact exactly which pre-existing behaviour changed rather than let a green suite imply nothing did. If you would rather rule differently, or want this split into its own request after all, say so now while it is cheap."}